\image(figures/empirical_variogram.svg)
// result_end

On clean Gaussian data both estimators track the true variogram closely. The method of moments is unbiased at short lags. Both drift slightly upward at long lags (by 0.03–0.05 at lag 7), where the field's spatial mean is estimated from the same data and few independent pairs are available. Their sampling SD grows with lag, from 0.05 to 0.25, so long-lag bins carry much less information than their large pair counts suggest. The Cressie–Hawkins estimator costs about 5–10% in SD on clean data.

Five percent outliers change the picture. Every pair that includes a contaminated value contributes \((4+\epsilon)^2\), so the method-of-moments variogram rises by about 0.75 at all lags. That looks like a large nugget, and a fitted model would assign most of the variance to it. The robust estimator removes about two thirds of the shift at short lags (0.55 against a truth of 0.30) and has half the SD. It is not immune to the outliers, but it keeps the shape of the variogram recognisable.

In the figure, the azimuth-135° variogram reaches the sill within about four units, while the azimuth-45° variogram is still rising at eight. The empirical points follow the true curves. The 45° points lie slightly above their curve because a ±22.5° sector also contains pairs in directions with shorter effective ranges. Directional variograms with different ranges but a common sill indicate geometric anisotropy, which a fitted model can remove by rescaling the coordinates.

>> Practical guidance

- Remove a large-scale trend before estimating the variogram (pass regression residuals). Otherwise the variogram keeps rising and is not bounded by a sill.
- Use lags up to about half of the maximum distance, and check the pair counts. Bins with fewer than about 30 pairs are unreliable.
- Use the Cressie–Hawkins estimator, or compare the two estimators, when outliers or heavy tails are possible. A large gap between them signals contamination.
- Check anisotropy with at least four directions before assuming an isotropic model. Narrow tolerances sharpen the directions but leave fewer pairs.
- Treat the empirical variogram as the input to a fitted model, not as a final estimate. Its bins are correlated and their precision falls with lag.

>> References

- G. Matheron (1963). Principles of geostatistics. *Economic Geology* 58(8), 1246-1266. doi:10.2113/gsecongeo.58.8.1246
- N. Cressie and D. M. Hawkins (1980). Robust estimation of the variogram: I. *Journal of the International Association for Mathematical Geology* 12(2), 115-125. doi:10.1007/BF01035243
- N. Cressie (1993). *Statistics for Spatial Data*, revised edition. Wiley. doi:10.1002/9781119115151
- E. J. Pebesma (2004). Multivariable geostatistics in S: the gstat package. *Computers & Geosciences* 30(7), 683-691. doi:10.1016/j.cageo.2004.03.012
